<%# Design preview: the trading floor's Change column.

    Built because the column shipped and was not found. It is there - the header renders and the cells
    render - but it is `hidden lg:table-cell`, so it appears only from 1024px up, and in this database seven
    of the ten active companies had no yesterday price, so the rows at the top of the table all showed an em
    dash. A column of dashes above the fold reads as a column that does nothing.

    Everything below is a mock built from the app's real table classes and the real `movement_class` helper.
    The figures are illustrative. %>
<% content_for :own_heading, true %>

<%# One row's worth of data per state. Plain hashes rather than a struct: nothing here renders a partial that
    expects a Stock, so nothing needs the interface. %>
<% states = [
     { company: "The Coca-Cola Company", ticker: "KO", price: 68.99, change: 6.39,
       note: "Up - green, an up arrow, and a + sign" },
     { company: "Carnival Corporation", ticker: "CCL", price: 21.85, change: -3.02,
       note: "Down - red, a down arrow, and a - sign" },
     { company: "Verizon Communications", ticker: "VZ", price: 40.12, change: 0.0,
       note: "Unchanged - slate, and no arrow at all" },
     { company: "Gone Away Inc.", ticker: "OLD", price: 5.00, change: nil,
       note: "Archived - the price is frozen, so there is no change to state" },
     { company: "Newly Added Co.", ticker: "NEW", price: 12.50, change: nil,
       note: "No yesterday price yet - the daily job has not run twice" }
   ] %>

<%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
           locals: { title: "Price change",
                     description: "Where the Change column sits, what each state looks like, and what it " \
                                  "does on a phone" } do %><% end %>

<%= render "components/ui/callout",
           tone: :info,
           title: "This is a preview of shipped UI, not a proposal",
           body: "The Change column is live on the trading floor. This page exists because it was not " \
                 "easy to find: it shows only from 1024px up, and most rows were showing an em dash " \
                 "because the companies had no yesterday price. Figures here are illustrative." %>

<div class="mt-6 space-y-10">
  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ where it is %>
  <section aria-labelledby="where">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="where" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Where it is</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        Third column on the trading floor, between <strong class="font-medium text-slate-900">Last
        price</strong> and <strong class="font-medium text-slate-900">Your holdings</strong>. As a teacher
        or an admin there are no holdings or actions columns - <code class="text-xs">StockPolicy#show_holdings?</code>
        requires a student with a portfolio - so it is the last column you see.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <div class="table-wrapper overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table-base table-fixed">
          <thead>
            <tr class="table-header-row">
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Company (exchange)</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right w-32">Last price</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right w-28 bg-blue-50">Change</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left w-32">Your holdings</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right w-52">
                <span class="sr-only">Actions</span>
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white">
            <% states.first(3).each do |state| %>
              <tr class="table-body-row">
                <td class="table-body-cell">
                  <span class="block font-medium text-slate-900"><%= state[:ticker] %></span>
                  <span class="block text-sm text-slate-600"><%= state[:company] %></span>
                </td>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right">
                  <span class="table-cell-content"><%= number_to_currency(state[:price]) %></span>
                </td>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right bg-blue-50">
                  <%# The arrow is here because it is in the app. An earlier draft of this page showed the
                      states list with arrows and this mock without them, so the two halves of a preview of
                      *shipped* UI disagreed with each other and one of them disagreed with the app. That was
                      reported before I noticed it. A mock's job is to be the thing. %>
                  <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-end gap-1 <%= movement_class(state[:change]) %>">
                    <% if state[:change].positive? %>
                      <%= lucide_icon("trending-up", class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>
                    <% elsif state[:change].negative? %>
                      <%= lucide_icon("trending-down", class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>
                    <% end %>
                    <%= state[:change].zero? ? "0.00%" : format("%+.2f%%", state[:change]) %>
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="table-body-cell"><span class="table-cell-content">You own 0</span></td>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right">
                  <span class="text-xs text-slate-600">Buy / Sell</span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">The tinted column is the new one; it is not tinted in the app.</p>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the states %>
  <section aria-labelledby="states">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="states" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Five states, and why two of them are a dash</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        Up, down and unchanged are three states rather than two - the ticker this replaced tested
        <code class="text-xs">&gt;= 0</code>, so a flat price was green with an upward arrow. The other two
        have nothing to state, and say so.
      </p>
    </div>

    <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: { padded: false } do %>
      <ul class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
        <% states.each do |state| %>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-3">
            <span class="min-w-0">
              <span class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900"><%= state[:company] %></span>
              <span class="block text-xs text-slate-600"><%= state[:note] %></span>
            </span>

            <span class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1 text-sm tabular-nums <%= state[:change] ? movement_class(state[:change]) : 'text-slate-500' %>">
              <% if state[:change].nil? %>
                &mdash;
              <% else %>
                <% if state[:change].positive? %>
                  <%= lucide_icon("trending-up", class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>
                <% elsif state[:change].negative? %>
                  <%= lucide_icon("trending-down", class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>
                <% end %>
                <%= state[:change].zero? ? "0.00%" : format("%+.2f%%", state[:change]) %>
              <% end %>
            </span>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ below lg %>
  <section aria-labelledby="narrow">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="narrow" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Below 1024px it is not a column</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        It collapses into the company cell, the way the holdings and the trade buttons already do. A third
        always-visible column would put this table back into a sideways scroll at 375px, which is the thing
        that was just fixed across the app.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-sm">
      <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: { padded: false } do %>
        <table class="table-base">
          <tbody class="bg-white">
            <% states.first(2).each do |state| %>
              <tr class="table-body-row">
                <td class="table-body-cell">
                  <span class="block font-medium text-slate-900"><%= state[:ticker] %></span>
                  <span class="block text-sm text-slate-600"><%= state[:company] %></span>
                  <span class="mt-1 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-xs <%= movement_class(state[:change]) %>">
                    <% if state[:change].positive? %>
                      <%= lucide_icon("trending-up", class: "size-3.5 shrink-0") %>
                    <% else %>
                      <%= lucide_icon("trending-down", class: "size-3.5 shrink-0") %>
                    <% end %>
                    <%= format("%+.2f%%", state[:change]) %> since yesterday
                  </span>
                  <span class="mt-1 block text-xs text-slate-600">You own 0</span>
                </td>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right">
                  <span class="table-cell-content"><%= number_to_currency(state[:price]) %></span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ buy and sell %>
  <section aria-labelledby="actions">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="actions" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Buy and Sell are still buttons</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        Unchanged by this column: `.tw-btn-secondary`, 40px, bordered white with a slate-700 label at
        10.35:1, rendered as links that open the order modal. They are **not** ghosts like other row
        actions, and not filled either - a filled teal Buy beside a filled amber Sell put two saturated
        fills in two hues on every row, and was reported as garish.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw-card p-5">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-6">
        <span class="text-xs text-slate-600">Active company</span>
        <div class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Buy</span>
          <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Sell</span>
        </div>

        <span class="text-xs text-slate-600">Archived - sell only</span>
        <div class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Sell</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        And no arrows on them, which is why the Change column can have them: a vertical arrow in a finance
        interface means the price moved that way, so the same glyph cannot also mean "place an order". The
        buttons used to carry one; the column now does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ why it looked empty %>
  <section class="tw-card p-5" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Why it looked like nothing was there</h2>

    <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Seven of the ten companies had no yesterday price</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          <code class="text-xs">yesterday_price_cents</code> is set by the daily price job, which has never
          run in this environment, and the column shows an em dash rather than inventing a change. The rows
          that did have one - BAC, CCL and F - were further down an unordered list, so the visible part of
          the table was all dashes. Every active company has a plausible change in the development database
          now, so the live page demonstrates itself.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">And it is hidden below 1024px</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          Deliberately - see above - but it does mean a narrow window shows no Change header at all, which
          reads the same as a missing feature.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">What I should have done</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          Said where to look and at what width, and populated the data before calling it done. A feature
          whose every visible row is an em dash has not been demonstrated, whatever the tests say - the
          tests create their own stocks with yesterday prices, so they passed on data the page did not have.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
